1. MPAS-Atmosphere (LANL / UCAR) -- BSD-3-Clause
==========================================================================

gpuwm-hex is a CUDA port of MPAS-Atmosphere (Model for Prediction Across
Scales) version 8.4.2. The port covers the dynamical core, the
init_atmosphere core (real-data and idealized initial conditions and the
static fields), incremental analysis update, limited-area boundaries,
restart, streams, diagnostics, and the MPAS physics driver layer
(mpas_atmphys_*.F) behind the selectable MPAS physics suites. Each ported
routine cites the MPAS file and lines it transcribes, and
tools/check_mpas_citations.py checks those citations against the pinned
source. The earlier v8.2.3 lane is still carried and selectable.

The Fortran oracles under tools/mpas_operator_oracle/ and
tools/mpas_step_oracle/, and the native-reference wrappers, patches and
test oracles under tests/ and tools/, transcribe or patch MPAS source. Those
are MPAS-derived portions too.

MPAS-Atmosphere is developed by the Los Alamos National Laboratory and the
University Corporation for Atmospheric Research / National Center for
Atmospheric Research. It is distributed under the BSD-3-Clause terms
reproduced at the end of this section. Those terms govern the MPAS-derived
portions of this distribution and travel with every copy of it.

That licence also requires that derivative works be clearly marked so as
not to be confused with the version available from LANS and UCAR. This is
that marking:

  - The distribution, the command and the namespace are named gpuwm-hex.
    The MPAS name is used only to state what this software derives from
    and what it is measured against.
  - Neither LANS, UCAR, NCAR nor their contributors endorse this
    software.
  - Results from gpuwm-hex are not results from MPAS-Atmosphere. Where
    the two are known to differ, the differences are measured, and the
    quantities are stated in README.md.

The default physics is ArWen's (section 10), not MPAS's. With it, the
cumulus scheme is a different generation from the reference: ArWen carries
the 2018 Grell-Freitas formulation, MPAS-A carries the 2013 ensemble fork.
They differ in closure membership, in the rain conversion coefficient and
its temperature dependence, and in whether shallow convection precipitates.
Three further differences of the ArWen physics are measured and declared as
divergences, judged against observations: an upper-band
potential-temperature drift, a convective rainfall deficit, and a
downstream condensate surplus. The MPAS physics suites (sections 2 to 6)
port MPAS's own schemes instead.

Two of the MPAS-derived operators carry a further upstream lineage, which
MPAS-Model distributes inside its own tree under the terms reproduced
below. The orographic gravity-wave-drag operator in
src/hexcore/cuda_gwdo_v841.py is a reimplementation of MPAS-Model's
physics_wrf/module_bl_gwdo.F, which is WRF-derived, and of
physics_mmm/bl_gwdo.F90, the Kim/YSU orographic gravity-wave-drag and
flow-blocking scheme from NCAR/MMM. Those files are cited by path and line
in the module that reimplements them.

The longitude arithmetic in src/hexcore/mesh.py
(_grid_rotate_convert_xl_longitude and reconcile_grid_rotate_longitudes)
reproduces the convert_xl formula of MPAS-Tools grid_rotate.f90, pinned by
source digest in that module, so that a rotated mesh's stored longitudes
can be checked to the producer's own rounding. MPAS-Tools is distributed
by LANS under the same BSD-3-Clause terms as MPAS-Atmosphere, reproduced
below; no MPAS-Tools source file is copied into this distribution.

